#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overview {#overview}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay augments classical machine learning pipelines with features generated "
"on quantum hardware. The function takes classical tabular data and returns a"
" set of quantum-transformed features. When a quantum system processes the "
"data, it generates rich features that capture nonlinear relationships "
"between the inputs. You can use the generated features on their own, or "
"combined with your original features, to train any classical model. On "
"small- and medium-sized datasets, quantum-transformed features can improve "
"the prediction quality compared to purely classical baselines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This function is built for the following users:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Data science teams with an existing machine learning pipeline on small- and "
"medium-sized tabular datasets, where prediction quality has plateaued. You "
"add Rimay as one additional preprocessing step, without building any quantum"
" circuits yourself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Researchers who explore quantum methods for machine learning tasks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Description {#description}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The quantum feature extraction algorithm [\\[1\\]](#references) combines two"
" principles of quantum machine learning. The first is quantum feature maps, "
"which embed data into a high-dimensional Hilbert space and therefore produce"
" more expressive representations. The second is quantum reservoir computing,"
" which uses the dynamics of a quantum many-body system as a nonlinear "
"transformation of the input.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay encodes the feature values and the mutual information between the "
"features into the local fields and the coupling coefficients of a spin-glass"
" Hamiltonian. Counterdiabatic quantum dynamics evolve the system toward its "
"low-energy states, which contain nonlinear mixtures of the correlations in "
"the input data. Rimay extracts these correlations by measuring single- and "
"two-body expectation values. The expectation values are the quantum-"
"transformed features that you feed into your machine learning model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Workflow {#workflow}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Rimay workflow: The training data defines the Hamiltonian, every sample "
"becomes one circuit, and the measured expectation values are returned as "
"quantum-transformed data](/docs/images/guides/kipu-rimay/workflow.svg "
"\"Rimay function workflow\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Preprocess your tabular data as you would for classical machine learning. "
"Rimay needs numerical features, so convert categorical features into "
"numerical ones, for example with scikit-learn's "
"[`OneHotEncoder`](https://scikit-"
"learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.preprocessing.OneHotEncoder.html).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Split the data into a training set and a test set, and upload both to the "
"function, as shown in the [Example](#example) section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay uses the training data to construct the Hamiltonian that represents "
"the data. Every feature corresponds to one qubit, and the full training set "
"decides which features, and therefore which qubits, interact. Rimay stores "
"this structure so that it maps the test data and later inference data with "
"the same Hamiltonian.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For every sample, the feature values set the local fields of the "
"Hamiltonian, and the mutual information between the features sets the "
"coupling coefficients of the structure from step 3. Rimay digitizes the "
"counterdiabatic evolution of this Hamiltonian into a circuit, and runs the "
"circuits on the QPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rimay measures the resulting bitstrings, then computes the single-body "
"expectation values and the two-body expectation values of physically coupled"
" qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You download the expectation values in a form that you can feed directly "
"into your model, or combine with your original features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"Because of this structure, the number of qubits that a run needs equals the number of features, and the number of circuits that it submits equals the number of samples.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After you fit a mapping, you can map new data through it, for example for "
"inference. Rimay then retrieves the Hamiltonian that it created during the "
"fit and builds the circuits for the new samples from it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Integrate Rimay into your machine learning pipeline {#integrate-rimay-into-"
"your-machine-learning-pipeline}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Pipeline with Rimay inserted between the train-test split and the "
"classical machine learning model](/docs/images/guides/kipu-"
"rimay/pipeline.svg \"Rimay as an additional preprocessing step\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As in a classical pipeline, you start by preprocessing the raw data, for "
"example by one-hot encoding the categorical features, and split it into a "
"training set and a test set. Instead of feeding both sets into your model "
"directly, you feed them into Rimay first, and train your model on the "
"quantum-transformed features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Benchmarks {#benchmarks}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Kipu Quantum benchmarked Rimay on the German credit dataset from OpenML "
"[\\[2\\]](#references), which classifies 1000 credit applicants as good or "
"bad credit risks. It has 13 categorical and seven numerical attributes. One-"
"hot encoding of the categorical attributes results in 48 numerical features."
" The benchmark holds out 20% of the samples as the test set.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As a baseline, several classical models were trained on the classical "
"features. With Rimay, a random forest was trained in two ways: on the "
"quantum-transformed features alone (Random forest Q), and on the quantum-"
"transformed features combined with the classical ones (Random forest H, for "
"hybrid). The table reports only the random forest, because it performed best"
" on the quantum-transformed features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Model | Features | Accuracy | AUC | F1 score | Precision | Recall |\n"
"| :-- | :-- | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: |\n"
"| [CatBoost](https://catboost.ai/) | Classical | 0.775 | 0.781 | 0.850 | 0.795 | 0.914 |\n"
"| [Random forest](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestClassifier.html) | Classical | 0.765 | 0.781 | 0.846 | 0.782 | 0.921 |\n"
"| [XGBoost](https://xgboost.readthedocs.io/en/stable/) | Classical | 0.760 | 0.758 | 0.835 | 0.803 | 0.871 |\n"
"| [LightGBM](https://lightgbm.readthedocs.io/en/stable/) | Classical | 0.770 | 0.751 | 0.840 | 0.818 | 0.864 |\n"
"| Random forest Q | Quantum | 0.750 | 0.793 | 0.840 | 0.762 | 0.936 |\n"
"| Random forest H | Hybrid | 0.770 | 0.798 | 0.854 | 0.770 | 0.957 |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The hybrid features improve all metrics of the random forest except the "
"precision, and the hybrid random forest reaches a higher AUC, F1 score, and "
"recall than CatBoost on the classical features. The largest effect is on the"
" recall, which rises from 0.921 to 0.957. The quantum-transformed features "
"alone do not reach the precision of the classical models.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"caution\">\n"
"  The test set holds only 200 samples, so predicting one more sample correctly moves the accuracy by 0.005. Read the differences as a trend that needs further verification, not as a significant effect. The prediction accuracy can also depend on the hardware-induced noise of the extracted features. Therefore, use enough shots that shot noise does not limit the result.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The benchmark used the following resources:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Example | Number of qubits | Number of samples | Number of shots | Total time (min) | QPU usage (min) | Processor |\n"
"| :-- | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: | :-- |\n"
"| German credit | 48 | 1000 | 5000 | 23 | 22 | ibm_boston |\n"
"| German credit | 48 | 1000 | 5000 | 3 |  2 | ibm_phoenix |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The number of samples, which is the number of circuits, drives the QPU time."
" Use the Nighthawk r2 processor type, because it greatly reduces the "
"required QPU time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Inputs {#inputs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `mode` determines which parameters a run needs. For a description of "
"every input parameter, see the [API "
"reference](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/functions/kipu-rimay#inputs).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Input files {#input-files}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Upload every file with `catalog.file_upload()` before you run the "
"function.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An upload replaces any earlier file of the same name, even for a job that "
"you submitted but that has not started yet. To run several jobs at the same "
"time, give the files of each job their own names, for example "
"`run1_x_train.csv` and `run2_x_train.csv`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every file is a CSV file with a header row. Every column of a feature file "
"is one feature, and every value must be numeric.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`y_train` holds exactly one numeric column, with one label per row of "
"`x_train`. Map categorical labels to numbers, for example good and bad to 1 "
"and 0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`x_test` and `x_inference` must have the same columns as `x_train`, in the "
"same order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"caution\" title=\"Keep the mapping_id and backend_name\">\n"
"  The `mapping_id` is the only way to reach the mapping that your fit produced, and it cannot be looked up or recovered. It authorizes the use of that mapping, so treat it like a credential and do not share it. If you lose it, you must fit a new mapping, which costs another run on quantum hardware. Also record the `backend_name` of the fit, because the mapping runs only on that processor.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Limits {#limits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every feature is mapped to one qubit, so the processor must have at least as"
" many qubits as your data has features. If you have more features than that,"
" reduce them with a feature selection technique first.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every sample becomes one circuit, and a run counts training and test samples"
" together. A run can hold at most 15,000 samples.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A single job can request at most 10 million executions, so the number of "
"samples times `shots` must not exceed 10 million.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A job must finish within 24 hours, including the time its circuits wait in "
"the processor's queue. A job that runs longer is stopped without a result. "
"On a busy processor, keep large datasets well below these limits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Outputs {#outputs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`job.result()` returns a dictionary. The quantum-transformed features "
"themselves are written to CSV files that you download with "
"`catalog.file_download()`. For a description of every key of the dictionary,"
" see the [API reference](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/functions/kipu-"
"rimay#output). For the time that each execution phase took, see [Run "
"metadata](#run-metadata).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every output file holds one row per input row, in the order of the input "
"file, and one column per quantum-transformed feature. The columns whose "
"names start with `corr_` are the two-body expectation values. The files hold"
" no label column. The labels of your training data stay in your `y_train` "
"file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run metadata {#run-metadata}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`resource_usage` holds one entry for each execution phase that the run went "
"through: `\"RUNNING: MAPPING\"`, `\"RUNNING: POST_PROCESSING\"`, and, on a "
"QPU, usually `\"RUNNING: WAITING_FOR_QPU\"`. Each entry holds `CPU_TIME`, "
"the wall-clock time of the phase in seconds, and `QPU_TIME`, the QPU time in"
" seconds. On a QPU, the QPU time that IBM billed is recorded under "
"`\"RUNNING: MAPPING\"`, the phase in which the circuits ran.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`\"RUNNING: WAITING_FOR_QPU\"` is the time your jobs waited in the IBM "
"queue. The run takes this time from the IBM job timestamps, and does not "
"count it in `\"RUNNING: MAPPING\"`. If IBM does not report those timestamps "
"for every job, the entry is absent, and the wait is part of `\"RUNNING: "
"MAPPING\"`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"IBM&reg; finishes its usage calculation some time after a job finishes. The "
"run waits up to about a minute for it. Usage that is still being calculated "
"after that time is not included in `QPU_TIME`, and the final usage is shown "
"on IBM Quantum Platform. If the run cannot read the usage from IBM, "
"`QPU_TIME` is `None`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For example:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Get started {#get-started}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Authenticate using your [IBM Quantum Platform API "
"key](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/), and select the Qiskit Function as "
"follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"  The following code assumes that you have saved your credentials. If you have not, follow the instructions in [Install the Qiskit Functions Catalog client](/guides/functions-get-started#install-qiskit-functions-catalog-client) to save your account with your API key.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Function sizes {#function-sizes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every job runs at one size — `S`, `M`, or `L`. If the specified size is too "
"small, the job is rejected. If the size is larger than needed, it runs on a "
"bigger compute profile than the work requires.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The size of a Rimay job depends on the number of samples it maps: the rows "
"of `x_train` and `x_test` for `fit-transform`, and the rows of `x_inference`"
" for `transform`. The labels in `y_train` do not count, and a `delete` run "
"maps no samples.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Size | Samples per job | Compute profile | vCPU | RAM |\n"
"| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n"
"| `S` | Up to 2,999 | `8x32` | 8 | 32 GB |\n"
"| `M` | 3,000 to 9,999 | `8x32` | 8 | 32 GB |\n"
"| `L` | 10,000 to 15,000 | `16x128` | 16 | 128 GB |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The number of samples is the only criterion. A size changes nothing but the "
"compute profile: the [limits](#limits) on features, samples, and shots are "
"the same for every size. `S` and `M` run on the same compute profile and "
"differ in the number of samples each covers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The default is `M`, used when a job does not request a size. A job of more "
"than 9,999 samples must therefore request `L`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can also read the mapping off the function itself with "
"`rimay.sizes_map`, and the size that a job runs at when it does not request "
"one with `rimay.default_size`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Calculate the size before submitting {#calculate-the-size-before-submitting}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run this on your own inputs. It reads them and does nothing else: no job is "
"submitted and nothing is reserved, so you can call it as often as you "
"like.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pass the data sets themselves, not their file names, in the same order as "
"`input_file`, together with the `mode` of the run. [Step 2 of the "
"example](#2-run-the-function) shows the call.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If the size is too small, the job fails with error `4600` and the message "
"names the size to use instead. If it is larger than needed, the job runs "
"normally and records a `4601` advisory event.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Example {#example}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example shows how to upload data, run the function, and retrieve the "
"quantum-transformed features. For an end-to-end example that includes the "
"preprocessing and the training of the classical model, see the "
"[tutorial](/tutorials/classify-credit-risk-with-rimay-quantum-feature-"
"extraction).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Upload the data {#1-upload-the-data}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example uses a small synthetic dataset with four numeric features and a"
" binary label, so that a run needs only four qubits and finishes quickly. "
"The next cell writes it to four CSV files: the training features, the "
"training labels, the test features, and the features for step 4 of this "
"example. To use your own data, write your preprocessed training and test "
"sets to files with the same layout instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Upload the training and test files. You upload `x_inference.csv` in step "
"4.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Run the function {#2-run-the-function}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Calculate the size of the run from the data that you uploaded, with the "
"calculator from [Function sizes](#calculate-the-size-before-submitting).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Select a processor with at least as many qubits as your data has features, "
"and run the function in `fit-transform` mode with that size. The sample data"
" has four features, so any IBM Quantum processor can run it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Check the workload's [status](/guides/functions-get-started#check-job-"
"status) or return [results](/guides/functions-get-started#retrieve-results) "
"as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Retrieve the results {#3-retrieve-the-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Download the quantum-transformed features. Pass `target_name` so that each "
"file is saved under its own name.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. Map new data {#4-map-new-data}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After you train your model, map new data with the fitted mapping, for "
"example to run inference. This example maps `x_inference.csv`, which the "
"first cell of this example wrote. Pass the `mapping_id` that the fit "
"returned, so that the new rows are mapped with the same mapping instead of a"
" new one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A mapping is built for the qubit layout of one processor, so pass the same "
"`backend_name` as the fit, which the fit returned in "
"`result[\"backend_name\"]`. Do not use `least_busy()` to choose the "
"processor, because it might return a different one. Rimay rejects any other "
"processor before it submits anything. The shot count also comes from the "
"fit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "5. Delete a mapping {#5-delete-a-mapping}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When you no longer need a fitted mapping, delete it. A `delete` run does no "
"quantum work, so it needs no processor, no shots, and no function size. You "
"cannot undo a deletion. After deletion, the `mapping_id` no longer refers to"
" anything, and a `transform` run with it fails.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Fetch error messages {#fetch-error-messages}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If your workload status is `ERROR`, use `job.error_message()` to fetch the "
"error message, as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every error that Rimay reports carries a code from the [IBM Quantum error "
"codes](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/errors), a message, and a solution that "
"names the value that you passed and the value that is allowed. The following"
" table lists the most common errors:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Code | Cause |\n"
"| :-- | :-- |\n"
"| `1221` | A parameter is missing or has the wrong type, `mode` is not valid, a file named in `input_file` was not uploaded, the files do not match each other, a feature is not numeric, or the `mapping_id` does not match a stored mapping. |\n"
"| `1221` | A `\"transform\"` run names a different `backend_name` than the fit that produced its mapping. |\n"
"| `1216` | Your instance has no processor with the `backend_name` that you passed. The solution lists the processors it does have. |\n"
"| `1200` | Your API key does not have access to the instance that the job runs under. |\n"
"| `3200` | `backend_name` does not start with `ibm_`. |\n"
"| `4500` | The data has more features than the processor has qubits. |\n"
"| `6000` | The number of samples times `shots` exceeds 10 million executions. |\n"
"| `6001` | `shots` is smaller than 1. |\n"
"| `6002` | The data has more samples than a run allows. |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
